### Inductive Switching Demonstration Sketch [SIMULATION ONLY]
> [!NOTE]
> **Simulation & Logic Demonstration Only:** This Arduino sketch illustrates software control timing (toggling a digital output pin HIGH and LOW). In physical hardware, Pin 9 controls the gate/base of an external switching transistor—never a direct motor coil! The software code alone does NOT absorb back-EMF or measure voltage spikes; electrical protection relies entirely on the physical flyback diode wired across the motor terminals.

```cpp
// [SIMULATION ONLY — LOGIC CONTROL DEMONSTRATION]
// In physical hardware, Pin 9 drives an external transistor switch,
// never a motor directly! Hardware protection requires a physical flyback diode.
const int motorControlPin = 9;

void setup() {
  pinMode(motorControlPin, OUTPUT);
  digitalWrite(motorControlPin, LOW);
  Serial.begin(9600);
  Serial.println(F("--- Transistor Motor Switching Logic Demo ---"));
}

void loop() {
  Serial.println(F("Logic HIGH: Transistor ON, motor spinning..."));
  digitalWrite(motorControlPin, HIGH);
  delay(1000);

  Serial.println(F("Logic LOW: Transistor OFF, flyback diode recirculates current"));
  digitalWrite(motorControlPin, LOW);
  delay(2000);
}
```

### Hands-On Simulation Task: Inspect the Clamping Action 🔬

Follow this structured investigation using the interactive simulator below:

1. **Step 1 — Initial Setup:**
   - Ensure the motor switch is **CLOSED / ON** and the flyback diode is toggled **OFF** (unprotected baseline).
2. **Step 2 — Predict the Unprotected Spike:**
   - **Predict:** What will happen to the voltage at the switch terminal when the circuit opens abruptly without a bypass path?
3. **Step 3 — Observe & Record Unprotected Turn-Off:**
   - Click **Open Switch** and observe: without a recirculation loop, the collapsing magnetic field creates an uncontrolled high-voltage transient that exceeds safe transistor ratings.
4. **Step 4 — Enable Diode & Compare Clamped Voltage:**
   - Toggle the flyback diode **ON** (connected antiparallel across the motor).
   - Click **Open Switch** again and record the result: the terminal voltage is clamped safely to approximately $V_{supply} + V_F$ ($\approx 5.7\text{V}$ under our 5.0V supply example).
5. **Step 5 — Trace the Recirculation Loop:**
   - Watch the animated current loop: verify that after turn-off, current continues flowing through the motor windings and loops back through the forward-biased diode, decaying to zero without crossing the open switch.
